WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:07.630
Hello, I am Dustin Kirkland a product manager
at Google and your host at this year's KubeCon

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:08.630
Copenhagen.

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:13.690
I am join by one of my colleagues Jan Tattermusch,
he is a software engineer at Google and a

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.299
real expert at gRPC load balancing.

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:23.010
Jan tell us a little about what makes gRPC load balancing
essential to high availably applications in

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.010
Kubernetes.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:29.779
Ok, so if I was to say it in a few words it's
basically load balancing is one of the key techniques

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:36.670
that allows you to build scalable applications
and one of the things that are maybe a little

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:44.390
bit less obvious also allow you to make your
services more reliable and to be able to do

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:45.990
things like live updates.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:46.990
Right.

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:50.790
That's a core piece of what happens in Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:51.790
Right?

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:56.130
Yes, so Kubernetes basically like you can't really 
not do load balancing when you are deploying

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.770
to Kubernetes.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.830
It's simply the right way to deploy applications
at scale?

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:09.649
Yes. Ok I understand you have a demo here
for us, you want to show us how this works?

00:01:09.649 --> 00:01:18.100
So what I have here is basically, so I have
already deployed a service that has three

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:26.909
replicas and I am going to show you that these
three replicas are here so you can see three

00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:35.810
end points and now I am going to deploy a
client on the Kubernetes cluster, so I am going to create

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:45.399
it based on my yaml definition i just created and when
I show you the logs you can see that the client

00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:51.600
basically reaches to all of the three existing replicas and it's doing the round balancing

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:59.859
using the balancer that is built into the gRPC client
itself so let's now see what happens if let's

00:01:59.859 --> 00:02:09.900
say two of my replicas die - it's going to
take a little bit - now you can see one of

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:16.390
the, some of the last backends
that we reached to its only the one last remaining

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:21.810
backend the other ones are unfortunate dead. 
They are not responding anymore?

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:25.270
So that is pretty much it.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:27.130
I could also easily scale up.

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:28.130
If I wanted to.

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:29.670
Let's get those back.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:33.240
It might take a bit longer then scaling down because, you know,

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:37.570
when they die they are immediately gone and
it might take a little bit for the DNS resolution

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:41.580
to pick up and to see those things replica
so I don't know if that is work immediately

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.680
But we can see a lot of our hits?

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:44.680
Ah there we are.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:49.880
So we were hitting 115 for a long time Ok.
And now we'll start to spread those across.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:54.170
to 116. and so we should be reaching all of
the 5 new replicas.

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:55.170
Excellent.

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:56.170
Wow.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:57.590
That looks really simple

00:02:57.590 --> 00:02:59.870
actually to set up.

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:05.350
I actually have some other examples that are
little bit more complex then this. They are

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:12.470
in a public GitHub repository that's open to everyone so I welcome everyone
to go ahead and try those examples.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:13.760
Great, thanks Jan.

